    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d172-a1">

                <pb facs="0381" n="281" sameAs="frus:frus1880/pg281"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 1 in No. 705.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Goldsborough to Mr. Seward.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Amoy</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1880-04-23"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">April</hi> 23, 1880</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 107.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In response to your dispatch No. 86, of
                    February 27, 1880, I beg to state that there are two private English schools at
                    this port for the education of Chinese, conducted by native-born Chinese, who
                    possess a fair knowledge of the English language, but there is no institution of
                    the kind founded or supported by the government.</p>
                <p>The missionaries have several schools of their own for the tuition of Chinese
                    boys and girls in the Chinese language.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>W. ELWELL GOLDSBOROUGH.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
